---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import WeeklyEffort from "../components/WeeklyEffort.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A tilted canvas on an easel, half-finished, beside paint swatches, a loaded brush and a loupe held up to inspect the work, in flat gold and black shapes on warm cream"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Every week you make something convincing, in one of four materials —
    paint, prose, currency or code — and then find out exactly where it fails
    to convince: the studio is built around that failure, not around the
    making. You'll age a surface by hand and learn what a lab test would
    still catch,
    copy a signature and watch its stroke rhythm give it away, and by the
    final project write a full provenance file plausible enough to survive
    questioning at a crit. You walk out with three finished pieces — a
    convincing copy, a written case against a sample you didn't make, and an
    object with a history built to match it — and a genuinely useful
    reflex: checking what a claim of authenticity is actually resting on.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    An elective for anyone who's sat through one semester of thinking
    critically about a discipline and wants to turn that scepticism into a
    hands-on craft. No prior studio, art-history or forensic background is
    assumed — the materials are taught from scratch, on the bench, in week 1.
    What the course does assume is patience for a slow craft skill and an
    honest appetite for being told, in public, exactly where your fake didn't
    hold up.
  </p>

  <WeeklyEffort />

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching session.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
